WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.220
- [Narrator] Most of
us have procrastinated

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.110
at some point in our lives,

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.790
but about one in every five
of us are compulsive about it.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:12.040
They put off tasks and chores
nearly every chance they get.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.460
They are called chronic procrastinators,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.490
and it turns out this behavior does

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.033
a lot more damage than you might think.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.760
We get it, doing work is hard.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.600
But when you choose to
watch TV over doing laundry

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.200
or washing dishes, you've just launched

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.150
an all-out battle in your brain.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.500
On one side, you have
your prefrontal cortex,

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.050
that's the part of your brain
that sets long-term goals

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.480
and regulates self-control.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.220
It's telling you that those dishes aren't

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.560
going to clean themselves.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.750
And on the other side
is the limbic system.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:49.980
It deals with pleasure,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.540
arousal, and reward, and it's telling you

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.990
that washing dishes is lame, dude.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.530
You'd have a better time
doing something else,

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.730
so procrastination essentially puts

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.930
your brain in its happy place.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:05.400
It feels good, that's why you do it.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.340
But of course, just because it feels good,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.340
doesn't mean it's
necessarily good for you.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.010
For example, several studies have found

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:16.270
that undergraduate college
students who procrastinated had

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.440
a lower GPA in the latter half

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.290
of the semester compared
to non-procrastinators.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.860
They were also more likely

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.360
to get sick based on
their healthcare visits.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.410
Moreover, other studies have found

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.260
that procrastinators report
higher levels of guilt

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.940
and anxiety when they choose

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.830
to procrastinate in the first place.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.610
And if you keep it up,
researchers have found

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.870
that chronic procrastination is linked

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:46.870
to low self-confidence,
low-energy, and depression.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.720
Overall, your quality of life
would probably be worse than

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.000
if you had just listened to
that little prefrontal cortex.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.070
And you may think that you
just have a different workflow

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.960
or you perform better under pressure,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.930
but sorry to say, there are no studies

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:08.490
to support any benefits of
chronic procrastination.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:13.300
Bottom line, it's unhealthy,
but not all hope is lost.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.950
In fact, researchers have conducted dozens

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.680
of scientific studies in search for ways

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.880
to help procrastinators.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.220
What they've found time

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.380
and time again is how you
think about tasks can make

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:26.750
a huge difference in how

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.960
likely you are to procrastinate on them.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.100
Tasks like saving for retirement

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.120
for example can be so abstract,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.630
there's no immediate deadline,

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.470
so you can always start tomorrow,

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:43.350
and this is the mentality that
lead you to procrastinate.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:47.680
Instead, make your tasks
more concrete in your mind.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.700
For example, a study in 2011 discovered

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.720
that people given an illustration
of how they might look

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.640
at retirement age were more

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.500
likely to say they would save money

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.330
for retirement than people
without an illustration.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.690
The image was something tangible,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.590
and therefore painted
a more concrete picture

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.050
of their inevitable future.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:12.060
So whatever the task,
do your health a favor

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.180
and do the work right now.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.330
You might even enjoy that TV show even

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.973
more once you get to it.

